  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Friday...\n\nToday will be the warmest day of the next 7 days. While wildfire \nsmoke aloft may reduce high temperatures by a few degrees in \nspots, increasing southerly winds ahead of an approaching cold \nfront will advect hot and dry air into the region, along with \ncontribute to some downslope warming over the plains of south- \ncentral Montana. This should yield high temperatures around 98 to \n108 degrees Fahrenheit today. With temperatures this hot, the \nExtreme Heat Warning remains in good shape with an expiration time\nof midnight MDT tonight. While Sunday will be another hot day, \nthe dry cold front passage tonight into Sunday morning will result\nin slight cooler high temperatures in the upper 80s to 90s, \nproviding at least some relief from the extreme heat. With that \nsaid, those sensitive to the heat should remain cautious Sunday \ndue to the cumulative effects of heat before much cooler \ntemperatures arrive for Monday and Tuesday. \n\nThis weekend, the heat and dryness combined with periods of \nbreezy winds and a wind shift from a cold front will result in \ncritical fire weather conditions over much of the area. The wind \nshift of concern is expected to occur tonight into Sunday morning \nas the previously mentioned dry cold front passes through the \narea. Please avoid causing a spark this weekend, and if you see a \nwildfire, report it.\n\nA much stronger cold front is then expected to arrive Sunday \nnight as a more potent shortwave trough moves through the region. \nThis front will bring much cooler temperatures for Monday and \nTuesday, along with some windy conditions. Look for high \ntemperatures to be in the upper 60s to lower 80s during this time,\ncoolest for most on Monday. As far as the wind goes, the \nstrongest north to northwesterly wind gusts are expected on \nMonday over far eastern Montana where gusts in the 20s to 40 mph \nare forecast and over far southern Montana into northern Wyoming \nin the Bighorn Basin and Bighorn Canyon where wind gusts in the \n30s to 50 mph are forecast. While the front will be mostly dry, \nthere is a slight chance for showers (around a 20 percent chance),\nand maybe a weak thunderstorm or two (around a 15 percent \nchance), late Sunday into Sunday night over central Montana \nassociated with a band of mid- level frontogenesis. Where this \nband sets up remains uncertain, but precipitation amounts are \nexpected to remain light with any precipitation that does occur. \n\nThe upper level flow pattern looks to remain somewhat cyclonic \ninto Wednesday before low amplitude ridging begins to nudge into \nthe area Thursday into the weekend. With this, temperatures will \nwarm back into mainly the 80s Wednesday and upper 80s to 90s \nThursday and Friday. While mostly dry conditions will continue, a \nfew subtle shortwave troughs may drop through the area from the \nnorthwest, allowing an occasional slight chance of precipitation. \nAtmospheric moisture values look to remain dry, with precipitable \nwater values below normal for the time of year. Therefore, would \nnot expect much for precipitation with any shortwave trough that \ndoes pass through the area.\n\nBelow is a summary of today's forecast high temperatures, with \ndaily and August records, at each of our main climate stations.\n\nSITE      8/1 FORECAST   8/1 RECORD      AUG RECORD\nBillings     105         102 (2008)    105 (8/2/2024)\nLivingston \t 104 \t\t 101 (2000)    105 (8/5/1961) \nMiles City \t 105 \t\t 104 (2008)    110 (8/7/1995) \nSheridan \t 104\t\t 103 (2008)    106 (8/7/1983) \nBaker\t\t 102 \t\t 99  (2022)    108 (8/12/2018)\n\nLA",
